# Installed as .github/workflows/lint.yml where there was no such file; with a
# workflow of your own, fold these steps into that one instead. Adapt the
# runner, the checkout action version and the language setup to the project;
# the load-bearing part is the last step.
#
# It invokes the AGGREGATE target. That is deliberate and it is the entire
# design: a workflow that lists sub-targets individually starts correct and
# decays, because adding a check to the Makefile and adding a step here are two
# separate edits and only the first one is ever remembered. Invoking `make
# lint` makes coverage total by construction — a check added to the Makefile
# runs in CI the same day, with no second edit and no way to forget it.
#
# If per-path job gating forces enumeration later, keep
# check-ci-lint-coverage.sh in the lint target: it is what notices the drift.
#
# This file is not a run. It triggers on a pull request and on a push to
# main, so once the first pull request is open, look at its run: the job below
# must have completed with every step run. No run at all (Actions disabled),
# or one that ends within seconds before any step (a billing stop), leaves
# pull requests unchecked and reports nothing. Then make this job a required
# status check on the default branch — the check is named after the job,
# `lint` — so that an unchecked pull request cannot be merged. With a merge
# queue, add `merge_group:` under `on:` first, or queued pull requests wait
# for a check that never starts.

name: lint

on:
  pull_request:
  push:
    branches: [main]

jobs:
  lint:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v7
        with:
          # check-ci-lint-coverage.sh compares the allowlist against the merge
          # base, so it needs more than the default single-commit checkout.
          fetch-depth: 0

      - uses: actions/setup-python@v7
        with:
          python-version: '3.x'

      - name: Run every check
        run: make lint
